<section class="section log-section" id="field-notes" itemscope itemtype="https://schema.org/Blog" aria-labelledby="notes-title"><p class="eyebrow">FIELD NOTES / 03</p><h2 id="notes-title">Working notes on technology and delivery.</h2><p class="section-lede">Short perspectives from my practice. These are viewpoints, not claims of measured client outcomes.</p>
<article class="note-entry" itemscope itemtype="https://schema.org/BlogPosting"><meta itemprop="author" content="Brandon Houle"><meta itemprop="datePublished" content="2026-11-23"><meta itemprop="mainEntityOfPage" content="https://brandonhoule.com/#note-workflow"><p class="entry-index">FIELD NOTE 01 / <time datetime="2026-11-23" itemprop="datePublished">23 NOV 2026</time></p><h3 id="note-workflow" itemprop="headline">Start with the work, then choose the AI</h3><div itemprop="articleBody"><p>AI adoption is a workflow decision before it is a tool decision. First name the work people are trying to complete, where time or uncertainty accumulates, and what a better result would look like. Then test whether a model, an agent, ordinary automation, or a process change belongs in that path.</p><p>A small trial should leave a record: the starting condition, the people doing the work, the exceptions, and the evidence that would justify continuing. If the result is not useful, pause or change the workflow. Activity alone is not progress.</p></div></article>
<article class="note-entry" itemscope itemtype="https://schema.org/BlogPosting"><meta itemprop="author" content="Brandon Houle"><meta itemprop="datePublished" content="2026-11-23"><meta itemprop="mainEntityOfPage" content="https://brandonhoule.com/#note-accountability"><p class="entry-index">FIELD NOTE 02 / <time datetime="2026-11-23" itemprop="datePublished">23 NOV 2026</time></p><h3 id="note-accountability" itemprop="headline">A human decision needs a named owner</h3><div itemprop="articleBody"><p>“Human in the loop” is incomplete until the person, decision, and handoff are clear. Teams need to know who reviews an exception, what information they receive, when they can override an output, and what happens when the system is unavailable.</p><p>That operating detail is part of the design. It makes accountability visible to the people using the system and gives leaders something concrete to inspect when risk changes.</p></div></article>
<article class="note-entry" itemscope itemtype="https://schema.org/BlogPosting"><meta itemprop="author" content="Brandon Houle"><meta itemprop="datePublished" content="2026-11-23"><meta itemprop="mainEntityOfPage" content="https://brandonhoule.com/#note-evidence"><p class="entry-index">FIELD NOTE 03 / <time datetime="2026-11-23" itemprop="datePublished">23 NOV 2026</time></p><h3 id="note-evidence" itemprop="headline">Measure the change the team can verify</h3><div itemprop="articleBody"><p>A useful scorecard separates what was observed from what is inferred. Task time, rework, exception rates, user confidence, and service quality each answer different questions. A time estimate does not become a financial return until a baseline and the conversion are validated.</p><p>Write down the measure before a pilot, review it with the people who do the work, and keep limitations beside the result. If the evidence is mixed, report that plainly. The next decision can be to expand, adapt, or stop.</p></div></article></section>
